    "https://www.hlg.edu/job_opening/34680/": {
      "title": "Professor of Media Communications",
      "description": "Professor of Media Communications | Hannibal-LaGrange University Request info Apply now Give Search for Search Position Number: 1.2.10.1 Date: 10/13/26 Position Title: Open Rank Professor of Media Communications Department: Media Communication Division: Arts & Humanities Number of hours per week for which this position is budgeted: 40 Number of months per year for which this position is budgeted: 12 Reports to: Vice President of Academic Administration FSLA Requirement: Exempt POSITION SUMMARY The Open Rank Professor of Communications is a full-time faculty member responsible for teaching and advising students in both the Media Communication major and Public Relations major at Hannibal-LaGrange University. The Communication Studies program at Hannibal-LaGrange has a long history of producing award-winning storytellers in both national and state-wide competitions. Hannibal-LaGrange students have consistently placed in the Baptist Communicators Association competition as well as the Missouri Broadcast Educators Association competition. The program offers practical hands-on training in a variety of fields to produce a well-rounded student who is ready to enter the workforce. Students gain practical experience through community partnerships with real nonprofit clients in the area. Students also produce a campus-based news show. Students have access to top-notch facilities and equipment such as a professional podcast studio, a news set, professional video equipment, and a mac lab. The candidate for this position should have experience in the industry in either public relations, print journalism, broadcast journalism, or radio. The ideal candidate should also have full-time teaching experience at the college level. In alignment with the University’s Christian mission and core values, the successful candidate will teach using a biblical worldview. The candidate must possess a vibrant, personal Christian faith including a clear testimony of Christian conversion and be actively involved in an evangelical, preferably Southern Baptist, church. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Teach undergraduate courses across communication disciplines, which may include courses such as: Introduction to Media Communication/Public Relations, Introduction to Audio and Video Production, Public Relations Strategies and Tactics, Media Law, Advanced Public Speaking/Debate, Broadcast Journalism, and Introduction to Public Speaking. Contribute to the ongoing development, assessment, and refinement of curriculum ensuring academic quality, educational relevance, and alignment with professional industry trends. • Mentor and advise students academically, professionally, and spiritually. • Incorporate faith-learning integration through teaching grounded in a biblical worldview. • Participate in university services including faculty meetings, campus visit days, committees, student learning assessment activities, and campus events. Support experiential learning opportunities such as internships, student media projects, or community partnerships that enhance student learning and community engagement. • Maintain awareness of emerging communication technologies, platforms, and best practices. • Fulfill other duties as assigned in support of the mission and academic goals of Hannibal LaGrange University. DEPARTMENTAL RELATIONSHIPS Academic Division Office of Academic Administration KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, AND ABILITIES Broad knowledge of communication theory and practice, including one or more areas such as digital communication, print journalism, broadcast journalism, media production, public relations, radio, etc. Ability to integrate theoretical foundations with applied, experiential learning approaches that prepare undergraduate students for professional and service-oriented careers. • Demonstrated commitment to effective undergraduate teaching, assessment, and student mentorship within a Christian higher education environment. Ability to integrate faith and learning through teaching grounded in a biblical worldview and consistent with the mission and core values of Hannibal-LaGrange University. • Strong communication, collaboration, and organizational skills are necessary for this position. EDUCATION, EXPERIENCE, AND TRAINING Required: Completed master’s or doctoral degree in communication, media communication, journalism, public relations or a closely related field OR a minimum of 18 graduate-level credit hours in communication, media, public relations, or a closely related field combined with relevant industry experience. Demonstrated ability or potential to teach effectively at the undergraduate level. Commitment to the mission, values, and Christian identity of Hannibal-LaGrange University. Preferred: Doctoral degree in a relevant field. Previous teaching experience in higher education. Professional or industry experience in public relations, journalism, broadcast journalism, radio, or digital marketing. To apply, please submit an application, including a cover letter here. Disclaimer * This job description is not intended to be all-inclusive; an employee will also perform other reasonably related job responsibilities as assigned by immediate supervisor and/or other management as required. HLGU reserves the right to revise or change job duties, required skills, or qualifications as the need arises. This job description does not constitute a written or implied contract of employment. The aforementioned job requirements are subject to change to reasonably accommodate qualified disabled individuals."
    },
    "https://www.hlg.edu/job_opening/head-coach-cross-country-and-track-field/": {
      "title": "​Head Coach, Cross Country and Track & Field​",
      "description": "​Head Coach, Cross Country and Track & Field​ | Hannibal-LaGrange University Request info Apply now Give Search for Search Position Number: ​1.7.7 ​ Date: October 19, 2026 Position Title: ​Head Coach, Cross Country and Track & Field​ Department: ​Athletics ​ Division: ​Athletics Number of hours per week for which this position is budgeted: 40 Number of months per year for which this position is budgeted: 10 Reports to: ​Director of Athletics​ Grade: ​Exempt ​ POSITION SUMMARY: The ​​​​Cross Country and Track & Field​ Head Coach​​​ is responsible to the ​Director of Athletics ​ . The position is ​Full-Time ​ and ​does ​ offer benefits, as specified in the handbook. ​​The coach will be responsible for all aspects of establishing and maintaining a quality and track and field and cross-country program upholding the mission and ideals of Hannibal-LaGrange University. ​ Employee must possess a vibrant, personal Christian faith including a clear testimony of Christian conversion and be actively involved in an evangelical, preferably Southern Baptist, church. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES: Responsibilities include all aspects of the program related to successful coaching, recruiting (including meeting roster goal), retention, budgeting, scheduling, promotions, fundraising, and mentoring along with any other administrative duties assigned by the Athletic Director Must maintain proper certification to drive HLGU vehicles – Missouri Class E driver’s license or other state’s equivalent Head Coach will be responsible for supervising any assistant coaches and providing oversight as necessary Act as a Campus Security Authority (CSA) Additional duties to be assigned DEPARTMENTAL RELATIONSHIPS Admissions, Financial Aid, Registrar, Institutional Advancement, Faculty, Business Office​ KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, AND ABILITIES Must have values consistent with the mission and purpose of Hannibal-LaGrange University. Must agree to, sign off on, and abide by the standards of employment on the employment application and in the employee handbook. EDUCATION, EXPERIENCE, AND TRAINING Master’s degree and previous coaching experience is preferred. To apply, please submit an application, including a cover letter here. Disclaimer * This job description is not intended to be all-inclusive; an employee will also perform other reasonably related job responsibilities as assigned by immediate supervisor and/or other management as required. HLGU reserves the right to revise or change job duties, required skills, or qualifications as the need arises. This job description does not constitute a written or implied contract of employment. The aforementioned job requirements are subject to change to reasonably accommodate qualified disabled individuals."
    },
    "https://www.hlg.edu/job_opening/graduate-assistant-womens-residence-hall-director/": {
      "title": "Graduate Assistant – Women’s Residence Hall Director",
      "description": "Graduate Assistant – Women’s Residence Hall Director | Hannibal-LaGrange University Request info Apply now Give Search for Search Position Number: 1.7.3.2 Date: 10/27/26 Position Title: Graduate Assistant – Women’s Residence Hall Director Department: Dormitories Division: Campus Life and Spiritual Formation Number of hours per week for which this position is budgeted: 20 Number of months per year for which this position is budgeted: 12 Reports to: Director of Campus Life Exempt: Non-Exempt: X POSITION SUMMARY The Women’s Residence Hall Director (RD) is responsible to the Director of Campus Life. The position is part-time. The RD is responsible for the overall operation of the housing units they have been assigned and the conduct of its residents, as well as oversight of Resident Assistants. Employee must possess a vibrant, personal Christian faith including a clear testimony of Christian conversion and be actively involved in an evangelical, preferably Southern Baptist, church. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Live in the dormitory for which they are responsible (may only have two vehicles including cars, trucks, golf carts, 4-wheelers, etc.) Maintain personal living space reflective of housing policies. (See Handbook) • Maintain office hours at least three times a week. Office hours must be posted. • Allow 3 to 5 hours of meetings each week with various Residence Life Staff (RAs and Director of Campus Life) Organize and oversee the Resident Assistant (RA) rotation schedule for on-call weeks. • Schedule one weekend off a month with the Director of Campus Life. • Maintain on-going contact with all Resident Assistants and residents. Work to resolve roommate issues. Oversee check-in and check-out procedures for residents. (Time off or vacation may not be taken during check-in and check-out times). Maintain records concerning room conditions, emergency contact forms, and housing contracts. Assess dorm damages and fines throughout the year. Conduct orientation meeting at the beginning of each semester to address housing policies and regulations. Turn in maintenance and housekeeping requests as needed as well as report these requests in a weekly report in staff meeting. Read, support, and enforce all HLG policies as outlined in handbooks. • Enforce the disciplinary action of those residents who violate college policy while in the housing units. Maintain records of disciplinary action taken and file a copy with the Director of Campus Life. Keep track of warning sheets for each dorm. Two warning system and then a fine is issued. Assist in supervising RAs assigned to the housing units. Create an RA schedule that includes the various duties of the RAs including nightly duties, on-call duties, visitation duties, posted hours and admissions tours, etc. (RA’s must be present at check-in and check-out dates) Monitor the work and performance of RAs through an evaluation process. • Other duties as needed or assigned by the Director of Campus Life. One weekend per month is allowed for overnight stays away from campus. • Summer vacation to be taken between June 1 st and August 1 st as approved by the Director of Campus Life (No RD will be approved for vacation during the week(s) of Super Summer). SUMMER DUTIES Check in camps and handle key issues. Assess and report damages to housing units weekly to the Camp Coordinator. • Assist with upkeep (could include cleaning, painting, or minor repairs, etc.) of the housing units and/or other campus facilities as assigned by the Director of Campus Life. Assist with housing issues as needed by the Director of Campus Life. DEPARTMENTAL RELATIONSHIPS Admissions Business Office Campus Security Maintenance and Housekeeping Retention and Career Services KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, AND ABILITIES Must have good interpersonal skills and be able to relate to college students. • Must have values consistent with the mission and purpose of Hannibal-LaGrange University. EDUCATION, EXPERIENCE, AND TRAINING Bachelor’s Degree required. Supervisory experience preferred. Must be accepted into an HLGU graduate program. TERMS OF EMPLOYMENT 2-3 year commitment required, depending on academic program chosen. • Free tuition for 18 credit hours of graduate course work per year (fall, spring, and summer terms). $500 monthly stipend + free on-campus housing (pending availability). To apply, please submit an application, including a cover here. Disclaimer * This job description is not intended to be all-inclusive; an employee will also perform other reasonably related job responsibilities as assigned by immediate supervisor and/or other management as required. HLGU reserves the right to revise or change job duties, required skills, or qualifications as the need arises. This job description does not constitute a written or implied contract of employment. The aforementioned job requirements are subject to change to reasonably accommodate qualified disabled individuals."
    },
    "https://www.hlg.edu/job_opening/professor-of-counseling-counseling-program-director/": {
      "title": "Professor of Counseling & Counseling Program Director",
      "description": "Professor of Counseling & Counseling Program Director | Hannibal-LaGrange University Request info Apply now Give Search for Search Position Number: Date: May 1, 2026 Position Title: Open Rank Professor of Counseling / Counseling Program Director Department: Academics Division: Professional Studies Number of hours per week for which this position is budgeted: 40 Number of months per year for which this position is budgeted: 12 Reports to: Vice President of Academic Administration FLSA Requirement: Exempt Start Date: July 1, 2026 POSITION SUMMARY The Open Rank Professor of Counseling and Counseling Program Director provides academic, administrative, and spiritual leadership for Hannibal-LaGrange University’s online graduate counseling program. This full-time, 12-month position includes faculty appointment at an open rank in Christian Counseling and carries primary responsibility for program development, enrollment growth, accreditation preparation and maintenance, curriculum oversight, faculty recruitment, and maintaining and fostering external partnerships. The Director will lead the Master of Arts in Counseling (MACO) program toward compliance with Missouri and Illinois licensure standards, develop a system for clinical partnerships in our region, and guide the program’s strategic development toward CACREP accreditation. As a faculty member, the Director will teach core counseling courses, mentor students, supervise clinical experiences as appropriate, and model integration of Christian faith with professional counseling practice. Employee must possess a vibrant, personal Christian faith including a clear testimony of Christian conversion and be actively involved in an evangelical, preferably Southern Baptist, church. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Program Leadership and Administration Provide overall leadership for the MACO program, including strategic planning, assessment, and continuous improvement. Oversee program operations, policies, and procedures. Lead curriculum design and revision to ensure alignment with Missouri and Illinois licensure requirements. Guide preparation for CACREP accreditation, including standards alignment and documentation. Accreditation and Licensure Serve as primary leader for CACREP research, preparation, and application. Ensure curriculum, faculty qualifications, and clinical hours meet accreditation and licensure standards. Monitor changes in state licensure requirements and recommend updates as needed. Faculty Leadership Teach core graduate counseling courses in the MACO program. Teach undergraduate psychology and counseling courses. Ensure faculty qualifications align with CACREP and institutional standards. Student Recruitment and Enrollment Growth Collaborate with admissions and marketing to promote the MACO program. Represent the program at conferences and recruitment events in Missouri and Western Illinois. Clinical Partnerships Develop and expand clinical placement networks in Northeast Missouri and Illinois. Cultivate relationships with community mental health agencies, churches, and counseling organizations. Faith Integration and Mentorship Model integration of Christian faith, ethical practice, and professional counseling standards. Mentor students academically, spiritually, and professionally. Other duties as assigned DEPARTMENTAL RELATIONSHIPS Work closely with the Online & Graduate Studies Department, Admissions, Marketing, Clinical Partners, and Academic Administration. KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, AND ABILITIES Knowledge of counselor education, counseling theory, and clinical practice. Understanding of CACREP standards and licensure requirements. Strong leadership, organizational, and communication skills. Commitment to Christian faith integration in counseling education. EDUCATION, EXPERIENCE, AND TRAINING Required: Earned doctoral degree in Counseling, Counselor Education, or related field. Doctoral candidates may be considered with clinical experience. Eligibility for counseling licensure in Missouri and/or Illinois. Experience teaching undergraduate and/or graduate-level counseling courses. Preferred: Experience directing or coordinating a counseling program. Familiarity with CACREP accreditation processes. Clinical counseling experience and online teaching experience. To apply, please submit an application, including a cover letter here. Disclaimer * This job description is not intended to be all-inclusive; an employee will also perform other reasonably related job responsibilities as assigned by immediate supervisor and/or other management as required. HLGU reserves the right to revise or change job duties, required skills, or qualifications as the need arises. This job description does not constitute a written or implied contract of employment. The aforementioned job requirements are subject to change to reasonably accommodate qualified disabled individuals."
    },
    "https://www.hlg.edu/job_opening/director-of-the-office-of-computer-services/": {
      "title": "Director of the Office of Computer Services",
      "description": "Director of the Office of Computer Services | Hannibal-LaGrange University Request info Apply now Give Search for Search Position Number : 1.3.4.1 Date: 05/11/2026 Position Title: Director of the Office of Computer Services Department: Computer Services Division: Business and Finance Number of hours per week for which this position is budgeted: 40 Number of months per year for which this position is budgeted: 12 Reports to: Chief Financial Officer FLSA Status : Exempt POSITION SUMMARY The Director of Computer Services is a hands-on systems administrator and technology leader responsible for the design, security, availability, and daily operation of the institution’s IT infrastructure and core services. Under the CFO, this role leads the planning and execution of network, server, cloud, endpoint, and Microsoft 365 environments; establishes standards and controls; and ensures dependable support for students, faculty, and staff. The Director supervises staff and coordinates with vendors. The Director manages priorities and budgets and actively participates in day-to-day administration, troubleshooting, and continuous improvement of campus systems. Employee must possess a vibrant, personal Christian faith including a clear testimony of Christian conversion and be actively involved in an evangelical (preferably Southern Baptist) church. Employee must abide by the policies and procedures found in the employee handbook. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Leadership, Operations & Strategy Under the Direction of the CFO, the Director will: Provide director-level leadership and accountability for reliable, secure, and scalable IT operations across campus. Translate institutional goals into technology roadmaps, lifecycle plans, and measurable service outcomes. Establish and maintain standards for system configuration, patching, backups, documentation, and change control. Prepare, manage, and monitor the technology budget, including renewals, licensing, and infrastructure refresh cycles. Manage contracts and relationships with technology vendors and service providers, ensuring performance, security requirements, and value. Systems, Infrastructure & Applications Administer and oversee network, server, virtualization, cloud, endpoint, and enterprise system environments. Ensure reliability, performance, and capacity through monitoring, patch management, configuration management, and proactive maintenance. Serve as an administrator for Microsoft-based environments (Active Directory/Entra ID, Microsoft 365, email, collaboration, device management) and identity and access management. Oversee enterprise applications (including the Ellucian Colleague Student Information System) and coordinate upgrades, integrations, permissions, and vendor support. • Own backup, disaster recovery, and business continuity readiness for core systems; test and document recovery procedures. Support institutional reporting and data access by maintaining secure data connections, roles, and technical documentation. Security, Risk & Compliance Implement and enforce approved security controls to protect campus systems, accounts, and data across on-prem and cloud services. Administer access controls (least privilege), account lifecycle processes, MFA/conditional access, and privileged access management as applicable. Lead cybersecurity readiness, vulnerability management, risk mitigation, logging/monitoring, and incident response coordination. Support compliance with applicable data privacy and information security requirements through policy, training, audits, and documented procedures. Personnel & Operations Supervise and develop Computer Services personnel; set priorities, provide technical leadership, and ensure effective escalation paths. Coordinate technology resources to ensure effective service delivery, asset lifecycle management, and clear communication with departments. Ensure support coverage for infrastructure, applications, and end users, including participating in troubleshooting and after-hours response as needed. Serve as the institutional representative to MOREnet and similar organizations; coordinate services, security posture, and technical initiatives. KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, AND ABILITIES Strong knowledge of enterprise infrastructure and systems administration, including networking, Windows/Linux servers, virtualization, backups, and identity management. • Experience administering Microsoft 365 and Microsoft-based infrastructure (AD/Entra ID, email, collaboration, endpoint management) in a production environment. Ability to lead technical staff, manage vendors, and run IT operations using ticketing, documentation, change management, and service level expectations. Strong communication skills, including the ability to explain technical risk, tradeoffs, and priorities to non-technical audiences. Customer-service mindset and commitment to the institution’s mission, values, and operational excellence. EDUCATION AND EXPERIENCE Bachelor’s degree in computer science, information technology, or related field required. • Master’s degree preferred. Minimum of 5 years of progressive experience in systems administration and IT infrastructure operations. • Supervisory or IT leadership experience (team lead, manager, or director), including responsibility for uptime, security, and incident response, preferred. To apply, please submit an application, including a cover here. Disclaimer * This job description is not intended to be all-inclusive; an employee will also perform other reasonably related job responsibilities as assigned by immediate supervisor and/or other management as required. HLGU reserves the right to revise or change job duties, required skills, or qualifications as the need arises. This job description does not constitute a written or implied contract of employment. The aforementioned job requirements are subject to change to reasonably accommodate qualified disabled individuals."
    },
    "https://www.hlg.edu/job_opening/office-manager-craigmiles-school-of-nursing/": {
      "title": "Office Manager, Craigmiles School of Nursing",
      "description": "Office Manager, Craigmiles School of Nursing | Hannibal-LaGrange University Request info Apply now Give Search for Search Position Number: 1.2.12.12 Date: 6/15/2026 Position Title: Office Manager, Craigmiles School of Nursing Department: Nursing Division: Academics Number of hours per week for which this position is budgeted: 40 Number of months per year for which this position is budgeted: 12 Reports to: Director, Craigmiles School of Nursing FLSA Grade: Exempt POSITION SUMMARY The Office Manager for the Craigmiles School of Nursing (CSON) reports to the Director of the CSON. The position is full-time and offers benefits as outlined in the Employee Handbook. Employee must possess a vibrant, personal Christian faith including a clear testimony of Christian conversion and be actively involved in an evangelical, preferably Southern Baptist, church. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES General Responsibilities Support and uphold the mission, philosophy, and policies of HLGU and CSON • Partner with HLGU to foster students’ intellectual and spiritual growth • Represent HLGU and CSON in a positive light when interacting with internal and external constituents CSON Operations Assist in the marketing and recruitment efforts of CSON Work in coordination with the HLGU Admissions Office to guide prospective, applying, and admitted students through both the university and CSON application processes, ensuring timely progression at each stage. Track and report applicant status. Maintain partnerships and contracts with healthcare agencies and other partners • Manage the calendar, correspondence, records, and files Serve as a liaison between CSON and Support Services on campus Oversee purchasing, inventory, invoices, and account reconciliation Supervise student workers and volunteers Evaluate administrative processes and recommend improvements Assist in the coordination and oversight of all major events of the CSON Support to the Director Manage calendar, communications, travel arrangements, and reporting tasks for the Director of the CSON Assist with accreditation and regulatory reporting Support budget tracking and program evaluation Coordinate faculty hiring processes, interviews, and onboarding Serve as point of contact in the Director’s absence Faculty Support Maintain curricular documents and program records Assist with committee coordination, meeting logistics, and documentation • Provide basic technical and administrative support Project Management Maintain key program documents (handbooks, evaluation plans) Coordinate preparations and approval processes required for all planned CSON student trips Manage all system operations required to initiate, maintain, and fully use the repository for generating meaningful data reports for ongoing program improvement KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, AND ABILITIES Strong organizational, communication, and multitasking skills Detail-oriented, adaptable, and professional Proficient in Microsoft Office 365 or similar systems EDUCATION, EXPERIENCE, AND TRAINING Bachelor’s degree preferred 2-5 years managerial experience preferred To apply, please submit an application, including a cover here. Disclaimer * This job description is not intended to be all-inclusive; an employee will also perform other reasonably related job responsibilities as assigned by immediate supervisor and/or other management as required. HLGU reserves the right to revise or change job duties, required skills, or qualifications as the need arises. This job description does not constitute a written or implied contract of employment. The aforementioned job requirements are subject to change to reasonably accommodate qualified disabled individuals."
    },
    "https://www.hlg.edu/job_opening/sports-information-director-part-time/": {
      "title": "Sports Information Director – Part Time",
      "description": "Sports Information Director – Part Time | Hannibal-LaGrange University Request info Apply now Give Search for Search Position Number: 1.7.1. Date: September 2, 2026 Position Title: Sports Information Director – Part Time Department: Athletics Division: Athletics Number of hours per week for which this position is budgeted: 20 Number of months per year for which this position is budgeted: 11 Reports to: Athletic Director FLSA Grade: Non Exempt POSITION SUMMARY: The Director of Sports Information is responsible to the Athletic Director. The position is part time and does not offer benefits, as specified in the handbook. The SID will provide media, website, home contest administration, and statistical oversight for all university athletic programs. Employee must possess a vibrant, personal Christian faith including a clear testimony of Christian conversion and be actively involved in a Southern Baptist church. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES: Oversee and be responsible for official statistics at all home HLGU athletic contests. This includes, but is not limited to, overseeing and coordinating all computer hardware and software that is necessary to operate statistic programs required by the conference and National Athletic Intercollegiate Association (NAIA). Provide live video streaming for all varsity home soccer, volleyball, basketball, wrestling, baseball, and softball contests. Includes set up and breakdown of event. Oversee quality public address system for all sports, which includes generating and revising scripts as necessary. Submit nominations for all applicable conference and NAIA awards on a weekly, monthly, annual or when needed basis. This includes but is not limited to athletic, academic, and character, individual and team awards. Submit and post all results/stats in a timely manner to local media, NAIA, HLGU website, and opponent’s media outlets. Maintain and ensure accurate and up-to-date information on the HLGU athletic website. Write and submit press releases within established guidelines after each contest, recruit signing, and other newsworthy stories. Coordinate with PR department to write stories that relate to the athletic department. (i.e., service projects by teams, individual and team honors, etc.) Supervise student workers and interns from Communication Arts Dept. • Other duties requested by the Athletic Director. DEPARTMENTAL RELATIONSHIPS Ability to work with various athletic department staff members to ensure quality media exposure for programs. KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, AND ABILITIES Must have efficient knowledge of athletic statistical software. Must have ability to write articles that will regularly appear in local media outlets and on HLGU athletic website. Must have values consistent with the mission and purpose of Hannibal-LaGrange University. Must agree to, sign off on, and abide by the standards of employment on the back of the application, on the yearly contract, and in the staff handbook. EDUCATION, EXPERIENCE, AND TRAINING Undergraduate Degree required To apply, please submit an application, including a cover here. (opens in new tab) Disclaimer * This job description is not intended to be all-inclusive; an employee will also perform other reasonably related job responsibilities as assigned by immediate supervisor and/or other management as required. HLGU reserves the right to revise or change job duties, required skills, or qualifications as the need arises. This job description does not constitute a written or implied contract of employment. The aforementioned job requirements are subject to change to reasonably accommodate qualified disabled individuals."
    }
  },
